% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 11, section 11.4, Example 7
%         ("General cause-and-effect pattern").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% The book writes these in the first person ("I will be rich if I buy a lottery
% ticket and my number is chosen"). We generalize "I" to *an agent* and avoid
% possessive apostrophes via "the number of *an agent*" and "the book of *an agent*".

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *an agent* will be rich.
    *an agent* will be famous.
    it will rain tomorrow.
    *an agent* buys a lottery ticket.
    the number of *an agent* is chosen.
    *an agent* writes *a book*.
    the book of *an agent* receives critical acclaim.
    *an agent* does a rain dance.
    the gods are pleased.

the knowledge base cause and effect includes:

an agent will be rich
    if the agent buys a lottery ticket
    and the number of the agent is chosen.

an agent will be famous
    if the agent writes a book
    and the book of the agent receives critical acclaim.

it will rain tomorrow
    if an agent does a rain dance
    and the gods are pleased.

scenario lucky is:
    bob buys a lottery ticket.
    the number of bob is chosen.
    carol writes novel.
    the book of carol receives critical acclaim.
    dan does a rain dance.
    the gods are pleased.
    riches expects answers ["bob will be rich"].
    fame expects answers ["carol will be famous"].
    rain expects answers ["it will rain tomorrow"].

query riches is:
    which agent will be rich.

query fame is:
    which agent will be famous.

query rain is:
    it will rain tomorrow.
